## The question you're answering

A growing e-commerce business finds that customer complaints are taking
too long to resolve, and different staff are often unaware of previous
contact with the same customer. Suggest one system the business could
implement to address this, and explain how it would help. (4 marks)

This is a Paper 2 business environment question and it's testing
something different to the data structures one we worked through in
M.1.1. There, the failure was missing Impact. Here, the failure is more
subtle. Students can write a fluent paragraph about *exactly the right
system* and lose the Name mark anyway, because they never actually
name it.

Read the question again. "Suggest one system" is not a hint. It is the
thing being tested. The question is asking you to put a label on what
you're proposing. If you describe the system perfectly but never
attach a name to it, the marker cannot award you the mark for naming a
system you did not name.

As in M.1.1, the answers below are highlighted to show structure
inline. The {% role name="name" %}Name{% /role %} is tinted green,
the {% role name="explain" %}Explain{% /role %} is tinted gold, and
the {% role name="impact" %}Impact{% /role %} is tinted orange. A
missing colour means the corresponding component is absent or too
weak to earn its mark.

## Answer One: the one that describes without naming

> {% role name="explain" %}The business could put in place a computer system that stores all the
> details about each customer and every time staff talk to them, so that
> anyone answering the phone can see what has happened before. This
> would help because staff would not have to ask the customer to repeat
> themselves and they would know what had already been said.{% /role %} The
> business would look more professional and customers would be happier.

Stop. Read that answer again. What did the student actually name?

Nothing. Not one technical term. They described a CRM, in plain
English, and they described it reasonably well. A marker reading this
sees a student who clearly understands the concept. But the mark
scheme says "Name one system." The student did not name one.

Let me walk through what a marker sees when this lands on their desk.

**Name.** No system has been named. "A computer system that stores
details" is a description, not a name. "Customer database" would be a
name. "CRM" or "Customer Relationship Management system" would be a
name. The student has dodged the naming and I cannot award this mark.

**Explain.** The mechanism described is roughly correct. Shared access
to customer contact history. Staff can see what has happened before.
That's half of what a CRM does, described in the student's own words.
Partial mark available here because the explanation is correct in
substance, even if the thing being explained has not been named.

**Impact.** "Customers would be happier. The business would look more
professional." These are generic business-good outcomes that could
apply to almost anything. There is no concrete consequence tied to
the specific problem the scenario described. The scenario said
resolution time was a problem. The answer does not say the system
reduces resolution time. The scenario said staff were unaware of
previous contact. The answer gestures at this but does not impact it
clearly.

What went wrong here is not that the student didn't know the content.
They did. They described a CRM from first principles. What went wrong
is that they wrote around the word rather than pinning it down. This
is the habit I see most in students who are strong at explaining
concepts in class discussion but weak at deploying them in writing
under pressure.

Marks this would earn: 1 out of 4.

## Answer Two: the one that names and stops

> {% role name="name" %}The business should use a CRM system.{% /role %} A CRM stores customer data and
> allows staff to share information. This would help the business.

This is the opposite pattern to Answer One. Where Answer One described
without naming, this answer names without describing. Both fail, for
opposite reasons.

**Name.** "A CRM system." There it is. One mark.

**Explain.** "Stores customer data and allows staff to share
information." This is a definition, not an explanation. It does not
engage with the scenario. It does not say how a CRM's mechanism
addresses the specific problems the question set up. Remember from
M.1.1: a definition floating free of the scenario does not earn the
Explain mark.

**Impact.** "This would help the business." You know this doesn't
earn anything by now. Same generic padding, same missing specificity.

Marks this would earn: 1 out of 4.

Same mark as Answer One, from the opposite failure. This is worth
sitting with. Students sometimes think a short answer is at least a
safe answer. It isn't. A short answer that names but does not engage
with the scenario scores the same as a long answer that engages but
does not name.

## Answer Three: the full four marks

> {% role name="name" %}The business should implement a Customer Relationship Management
> (CRM) system.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}A CRM holds a single shared record for each customer
> with every previous contact logged against it, so any member of
> staff picking up a complaint can see the full history of prior
> conversations without having to ask the customer to repeat
> themselves.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}This means resolution times drop because staff start
> each call with the context they need rather than gathering it, and
> customers are not forced to re-explain problems they have already
> reported, which directly addresses the two failure modes the
> scenario identifies.{% /role %}

Walk through it.

**Name.** "A Customer Relationship Management (CRM) system." Named.
Full name given once, abbreviation available for later reference.
Mark earned cleanly.

**Explain.** "A CRM holds a single shared record for each customer
with every previous contact logged against it, so any member of staff
picking up a complaint can see the full history of prior
conversations." This explains the mechanism (shared record, logged
contact history) and ties it to the scenario (any member of staff
picking up a complaint). The explanation is not a generic textbook
entry; it is a targeted answer to the question asked. Mark earned.

**Impact.** This is where the answer separates from a 3/4. It does
two things. First, it gives a concrete technical consequence:
"resolution times drop because staff start each call with the context
they need rather than gathering it." Second, it closes the loop
against the specific language of the question: "directly addresses
the two failure modes the scenario identifies." The student has
pointed at the question's own wording to show they answered what was
asked. Markers notice this. It is the cleanest signal a candidate
can send that they engaged with the specific scenario rather than
delivering a pre-cached answer.

Marks this would earn: 4 out of 4.

## What to take from all three

The lesson here is a different one to M.1.1. That topic taught you
to write the Impact. This one teaches you to write the Name.

When you sit down to write a suggested-system answer, do this
explicitly. Before you write a single sentence, write the name of
the system in capital letters at the top of your rough paper. CRM.
ERP. SCM. MIS. Whatever it is. Then write your answer with the
name used in the first sentence. No dancing around it. No
describing it into existence. Name it, then explain how it works,
then impact the specific problems the scenario gave you.

The test you can run on your own answer before you submit it is
simple. If you removed every noun phrase from your answer and
replaced them with "the system" or "this thing," would the answer
still mean anything? If the answer reads almost the same with
generic placeholders, you have described without naming. Go back
and put the proper names in.
